/* ---- swing choreography ----
   A swing is four beats, not one sweep: the anticipation that telegraphs it,
   the strike, the follow-through that carries the weapon's weight past the
   target, and the recovery back to guard. Before this it was a single eased
   lerp from one pose to another, which is why heavy weapons had no weight and
   light ones had no snap.

   Amplitude and timing come from the weapon's own measured geometry -- how long
   the blade is and how much of it there is -- so a greatsword takes a wider,
   later, heavier arc than a dagger without either being hand-authored twice,
   and a weapon added later inherits the right feel from its shape.

   `reach` below is the one rule that keeps a weapon out of its owner. The torso
   is barely half a unit deep, and every self-intersection in the game was the
   middle of a long weapon crossing the chest. Pushing the hand forward while
   the edge is anywhere near body height fixes all of them at once, and reads as
   a fighter holding the thing out in front of themselves rather than hugging it.
*/
/* ---- swing choreography ----
   A swing is four beats, not one sweep: the anticipation that telegraphs it,
   the strike, the follow-through that carries the weapon's weight past the
   target, and the recovery back to guard. Before this it was a single eased
   lerp between two poses, which is why heavy weapons had no weight and light
   ones had no snap.

   Every beat is an explicit POSE the weapon is allowed to be in, blended
   between. That matters for more than feel: the old version added a fixed
   offset to whatever the resting grip happened to be, so a weapon carried
   tilted back over the shoulder got that offset piled on top and was rotated
   straight down through its owner's chest. A pose that is stated outright
   cannot do that.

   Amplitude and timing still come from the weapon's own measured geometry, so a
   greatsword takes a wider, later, heavier arc than a dagger sharing the same
   style, and a weapon added later inherits the right feel from its shape. */
function keyed(p,keys){
  for(var i=1;i<keys.length;i++){
    if(p<=keys[i][0]||i===keys.length-1){
      var t0=keys[i-1][0],t1=keys[i][0];
      var u=clamp((p-t0)/Math.max(1e-6,t1-t0),0,1);
      u=u*u*(3-2*u);                                  // ease in and out of every key
      return keys[i-1][1]+(keys[i][1]-keys[i-1][1])*u;
    }
  }
  return keys[keys.length-1][1];
}
